actionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:42:36.0523851+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Weakness in SSO Authorization</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Elevation Of Privilege</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>A user subject gains increased capability or privilege by taking advantage of an implementation bug.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by enforcing PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange) for the OAuth2 flow and requiring mandatory HTTPS (TLS 1.2+) for all token exchanges. This prevents authorization code injection and interception. We also use Short-lived Access Tokens and Rotation of Refresh Tokens to limit the window of opportunity for an attacker. Additionally, the system implements Strict Redirect URI Validation to prevent token leakage to malicious domains. This is verified using DAST (OWASP ZAP) to check for protocol vulnerabilities and Snyk (SCA) to ensure the SSO libraries used are up to date and free of known vulnerabilities like 'Covert Redirect</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>f3ecc179-5b6d-402b-b9e1-54d50d10a835</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>b20a7821-af75-4c7a-a205-bce61a49c676</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>E3</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7.1b20a7821-af75-4c7a-a205-bce61a49c6764a2c10be-a517-4b46-8762-bf7d658160c2a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>4a2c10be-a517-4b46-8762-bf7d658160c2</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>1</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>b20a7821-af75-4c7a-a205-bce61a49c676:4a2c10be-a517-4b46-8762-bf7d658160c2:a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:16:07.7710398+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Destination Data Store Non Relational Database</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing mTLS (Mutual TLS) and Strong Authentication (Username/Password combined with IP Whitelisting). Connection strings within the application code are fully encrypted and retrieved securely from GitHub Secrets to prevent an attacker from spoofing the destination database. Verification is performed using a SAST (Bandit) scan to identify any insecure database connection attempts.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>b20a7821-af75-4c7a-a205-bce61a49c676</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7.1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7ac7c32ba-85d0-4f94-a0c0-22235cf1f5d500a564d9-b05d-46d5-ae0f-8038de43ab69b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>00a564d9-b05d-46d5-ae0f-8038de43ab69</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>2</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>ac7c32ba-85d0-4f94-a0c0-22235cf1f5d5:00a564d9-b05d-46d5-ae0f-8038de43ab69:b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:17:29.5526553+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Source Data Store Payment gateway </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Webhook Signature Verification. Every response sent from the Payment Gateway to Cart Management is digitally signed using a secret key. Our system recalculates the HMAC signature and compares it with the one provided in the header to ensure the data is coming from the authentic provider. Additionally, we use IP Whitelisting to only accept requests from known Payment Gateway IP ranges, which is verified during DAST testing.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>ac7c32ba-85d0-4f94-a0c0-22235cf1f5d5</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7.1ac7c32ba-85d0-4f94-a0c0-22235cf1f5d500a564d9-b05d-46d5-ae0f-8038de43ab69b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>00a564d9-b05d-46d5-ae0f-8038de43ab69</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>3</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>ac7c32ba-85d0-4f94-a0c0-22235cf1f5d5:00a564d9-b05d-46d5-ae0f-8038de43ab69:b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:18:15.1316234+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Destination Data Store Cart Management </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing JWT (JSON Web Tokens) and mTLS (Mutual TLS) for all internal service communication. When a source service sends data to Cart Management, it must verify the destination's digital certificate. Furthermore, Cart Management requires a valid service-token for every request. This ensures that data is only written to the authentic Cart Management service and not a spoofed target. This configuration is verified through SAST (to check for secure communication protocols in code) and Cloud Infrastructure Audits.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>ac7c32ba-85d0-4f94-a0c0-22235cf1f5d5</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7.1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860a6f60be1-99cb-433e-98aa-ccfff31e04187f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>a6f60be1-99cb-433e-98aa-ccfff31e0418</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>4</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860:a6f60be1-99cb-433e-98aa-ccfff31e0418:7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:19:03.5065528+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Source Data Store Cart Management </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Service-to-Service Authentication using JWT (JSON Web Tokens). The Product Catalog is configured to validate the digital signature of the 'Source' (Cart Management) on every request. Any data delivered without a valid, signed token is automatically rejected. Furthermore, we use mTLS (Mutual TLS) to encrypt the communication channel, ensuring that only authenticated 'Source' nodes can establish a connection. This is verified using SAST tools to check the implementation of token validation logic in the middleware</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7.1b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860a6f60be1-99cb-433e-98aa-ccfff31e04187f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>a6f60be1-99cb-433e-98aa-ccfff31e0418</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>5</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860:a6f60be1-99cb-433e-98aa-ccfff31e0418:7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:19:59.4950902+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Destination Data Store Product Catalog </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by enforcing Server-Side Identity Verification through TLS Certificate Validation. The system is configured to verify the destination's SSL/TLS certificate against a trusted Root Certificate Authority (CA) before any data is transmitted. Furthermore, the destination URL for the Product Catalog is stored in a Secure Environment Variable and is hardcoded to use HTTPS only. This prevents a 'Man-in-the-Middle' attacker from redirecting data to a malicious target. We verify this implementation using SAST scanning to ensure no insecure (HTTP) protocols are used in the communication logic</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7.1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7.1a752a785-1c70-4252-93fb-b1e12fd7420eebd1011c-2ac9-4fb7-8638-df95fde9ac5665bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695a</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>ebd1011c-2ac9-4fb7-8638-df95fde9ac56</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>6</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>a752a785-1c70-4252-93fb-b1e12fd7420e:ebd1011c-2ac9-4fb7-8638-df95fde9ac56:65bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695a</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:21:58.9190319+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Destination Data Store Payment Processing </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Service-to-Service Authentication using mTLS (Mutual TLS). The application is configured to verify the TLS Certificate of the 'Payment Processing' destination before transmitting any sensitive data. Furthermore, we use API Key rotation and secure endpoint whitelisting stored in GitHub Secrets to ensure data is only sent to the verified production environment. This implementation is verified using SAST (Static Analysis) to check for secure communication protocols and Cloud Security Audits to ensure only authorized endpoints are accessible</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>a752a785-1c70-4252-93fb-b1e12fd7420e</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>65bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695a</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7.1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b7086046dfb60c-c611-4c18-9488-0db1a167f103b20a7821-af75-4c7a-a205-bce61a49c676</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>46dfb60c-c611-4c18-9488-0db1a167f103</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>7</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860:46dfb60c-c611-4c18-9488-0db1a167f103:b20a7821-af75-4c7a-a205-bce61a49c676</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:22:47.4121773+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Source Data Store Cart Management </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Service-to-Service Authentication using Signed JWTs (JSON Web Tokens). The Authorization Provider is configured to only accept requests that include a valid digital signature from the Cart Management service. We also use mTLS (Mutual TLS) to ensure that the communication channel itself is authenticated between these two specific nodes. This implementation is verified using SAST (Static Analysis) to check the token validation middleware and SCA to ensure the encryption libraries are secure</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Response </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>b20a7821-af75-4c7a-a205-bce61a49c676</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>I23b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b7086046dfb60c-c611-4c18-9488-0db1a167f103b20a7821-af75-4c7a-a205-bce61a49c676</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>46dfb60c-c611-4c18-9488-0db1a167f103</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>8</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860:46dfb60c-c611-4c18-9488-0db1a167f103:b20a7821-af75-4c7a-a205-bce61a49c676</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:23:30.0837513+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Weak Access Control for a Resource</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Information Disclosure</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Information disclosure happens when the information can be read by an unauthorized party.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC) and Data Encryption at Rest using AES-256. Access to Cart Management resources is restricted to the authenticated owner of the cart and authorized administrative services only. Furthermore, sensitive fields are masked in application logs to prevent accidental data leakage. We verify this configuration using SAST (to ensure encryption is correctly implemented in the code) and Gitleaks (to ensure no sensitive access keys are committed to the repository)</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Response </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>b20a7821-af75-4c7a-a205-bce61a49c676</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>I23</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>E3b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b7086046dfb60c-c611-4c18-9488-0db1a167f103b20a7821-af75-4c7a-a205-bce61a49c676</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>46dfb60c-c611-4c18-9488-0db1a167f103</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>9</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860:46dfb60c-c611-4c18-9488-0db1a167f103:b20a7821-af75-4c7a-a205-bce61a49c676</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:24:00.0409427+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Weakness in SSO Authorization</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Elevation Of Privilege</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>A user subject gains increased capability or privilege by taking advantage of an implementation bug.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by enforcing OAuth2 with PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange) to prevent authorization code injection and MitM attacks. We have implemented Strict SSL/TLS (HTTPS) with certificate pinning to secure tokens in transit. Furthermore, we use SCA (Software Composition Analysis) to ensure the SSO libraries are patched against known CVEs and SAST to verify that redirect_uri parameters are strictly whitelisted in the source code</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Response </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>b20a7821-af75-4c7a-a205-bce61a49c676</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>E3</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1fe2a1175f-d86e-4e92-b429-bfa8442762b97f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>e2a1175f-d86e-4e92-b429-bfa8442762b9</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>18</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f:e2a1175f-d86e-4e92-b429-bfa8442762b9:7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:26:30.4876386+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Source Data Store Non Relational Database</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Database Authentication using Strong Credentials and mTLS (Mutual TLS). The Product Catalog service is configured to authenticate the database's identity using a trusted CA Certificate before accepting any data. Furthermore, we use IAM (Identity and Access Control) roles to ensure that only the verified database instance can communicate with the Product Catalog. This is verified using SAST tools to ensure that 'Certificate Validation' is not disabled in the database driver configuration and Secret Scanning to ensure database keys are managed securely via GitHub Secrets.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7.17f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e7b98748f-8c40-487e-8620-7e42e1051db3a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>7b98748f-8c40-487e-8620-7e42e1051db3</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>17</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e:7b98748f-8c40-487e-8620-7e42e1051db3:a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:25:57.3648362+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Destination Data Store Non Relational Database</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>his threat is mitigated by implementing Certificate-Based Authentication and TLS Encryption. The application is configured to verify the database's SSL/TLS Certificate against a trusted Certificate Authority (CA) before any connection is established. Furthermore, we use Secure Connection Strings stored in GitHub Secrets that include the specific hostname and port of our database, preventing 'Destination Spoofing' through DNS poisoning. This implementation is verified using SAST (Static Analysis) to ensure that the code enforces strict certificate validation and does not allow 'allow-all' or insecure connections</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Response </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7.1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S77f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e7b98748f-8c40-487e-8620-7e42e1051db3a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>7b98748f-8c40-487e-8620-7e42e1051db3</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>16</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e:7b98748f-8c40-487e-8620-7e42e1051db3:a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:25:18.3750095+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Source Data Store Product Catalog </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Database Access Control Lists (ACLs) and Service-level Authentication. The Non-Relational Database is configured to only accept write-requests that carry a valid Identity Token or API Key assigned specifically to the Product Catalog service. Furthermore, we use IP Whitelisting at the database firewall level to ensure that only the Product Catalog’s container IP can communicate with it. This is verified using SAST to ensure database credentials are not hardcoded and Cloud Security Audits to verify firewall rules</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Response </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7.1b20a7821-af75-4c7a-a205-bce61a49c6765c1a3eaf-4124-4ed8-bcb7-beb7084932feb89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>5c1a3eaf-4124-4ed8-bcb7-beb7084932fe</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>13</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>b20a7821-af75-4c7a-a205-bce61a49c676:5c1a3eaf-4124-4ed8-bcb7-beb7084932fe:b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:24:43.287016+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Destination Data Store Cart Management </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Service-to-Service Authentication through mTLS (Mutual TLS). Every request sent to the Cart Management service requires the destination to present a valid SSL/TLS certificate signed by our internal Certificate Authority (CA). Additionally, the destination URL is stored in Environment Variables (managed via GitHub Secrets) to prevent hardcoded URL manipulation. We verify this via SAST (Static Analysis) to ensure the communication libraries enforce certificate validation and do not allow insecure connections</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>b20a7821-af75-4c7a-a205-bce61a49c676</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7.1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7.1a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1fe2a1175f-d86e-4e92-b429-bfa8442762b97f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>e2a1175f-d86e-4e92-b429-bfa8442762b9</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>19</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f:e2a1175f-d86e-4e92-b429-bfa8442762b9:7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:27:11.2512216+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Destination Data Store Product Catalog </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Endpoint Authentication and TLS Certificate Pinning. The application is configured to verify the destination's SSL certificate against a trusted internal CA before any data transmission occurs. Furthermore, the Product Catalog's destination URL is fetched from Secure Environment Variables (GitHub Secrets), ensuring that an attacker cannot redirect traffic via local configuration tampering. This is verified using SAST scanning to ensure no 'allow-all' or insecure HTTP protocols are used in the service-to-service communication logic.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7.1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S765bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695ac74079c8-0572-4203-8684-4350670b290f3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>c74079c8-0572-4203-8684-4350670b290f</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>20</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>65bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695a:c74079c8-0572-4203-8684-4350670b290f:3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:27:51.4434444+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Source Data Store Payment Processing </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Service-to-Service Authentication using Signed HMAC (Hash-based Message Authentication Code) or JWT. The Order Database only accepts updates from the Payment Processing service if the request contains a valid digital signature. Additionally, we use IP Whitelisting at the network layer and Identity-based Access Control (IAM) to ensure only the Payment Processing container can connect to the database. This implementation is verified using SAST (to check for secure signature validation) and Secret Scanning (to ensure the shared keys are stored securely in GitHub Secrets)</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>65bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695a</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7.165bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695ac74079c8-0572-4203-8684-4350670b290f3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>c74079c8-0572-4203-8684-4350670b290f</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>21</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>65bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695a:c74079c8-0572-4203-8684-4350670b290f:3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:28:42.3254565+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Destination Data Store Order Database </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Certificate-Based Authentication and TLS Encryption. The application is configured to verify the database's SSL/TLS Certificate against a trusted internal Certificate Authority (CA) before establishing a connection. Furthermore, we use Secure Connection Strings managed via GitHub Secrets that specify the exact hostname and port of our production database, preventing 'Destination Spoofing' through DNS hijacking. This implementation is verified using SAST (Static Analysis) to ensure that the code enforces strict certificate validation and rejects insecure or untrusted connections</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>65bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695a</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7.1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7.165bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695ab009d8f8-14e4-41f1-a10d-b206bf5af63ab89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>b009d8f8-14e4-41f1-a10d-b206bf5af63a</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>29</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>65bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695a:b009d8f8-14e4-41f1-a10d-b206bf5af63a:b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:31:14.0061945+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Destination Data Store Cart Management </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Service Identity Verification through mTLS (Mutual TLS). The sending service is configured to verify the TLS Certificate presented by the Cart Management service against a trusted internal Root CA before data transmission occurs. Furthermore, we use HSTS (HTTP Strict Transport Security) to prevent protocol downgrade attacks and ensure all communication happens over encrypted channels. This implementation is verified using SAST (to check for strict certificate validation logic) and Cloud Security Audits to ensure the internal network DNS cannot be spoofed to redirect traffic to unauthorized targets</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Response </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>65bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695a</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7.1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S765bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695ab009d8f8-14e4-41f1-a10d-b206bf5af63ab89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>b009d8f8-14e4-41f1-a10d-b206bf5af63a</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>28</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>65bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695a:b009d8f8-14e4-41f1-a10d-b206bf5af63a:b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:30:35.0985525+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Source Data Store Payment Processing </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Webhook Signature Verification using a shared secret key. Every response sent from Payment Processing to Cart Management is digitally signed (HMAC-SHA256). Cart Management recalculates the signature upon receipt to verify the sender's identity before updating any data. Additionally, the communication is protected by mTLS (Mutual TLS), ensuring that only the authenticated Payment Processing service can establish a connection. This implementation is verified using SAST (to check signature logic) and DAST (to attempt unauthorized data delivery).</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Response </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>65bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695a</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7.17f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130ea5b90cdd-b31b-4e97-a152-48ecdda0f4703d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>a5b90cdd-b31b-4e97-a152-48ecdda0f470</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>31</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e:a5b90cdd-b31b-4e97-a152-48ecdda0f470:3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:32:19.6183702+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Destination Data Store Order Database </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by enforcing TLS 1.3 with Server Certificate Validation. The connection string is configured to require a valid SSL certificate signed by our internal Certificate Authority (CA). If the destination presents a self-signed or invalid certificate, the connection is immediately dropped. We also use Network Isolation (VPC/Subnetting) to ensure the database is not accessible from the public internet, preventing external DNS spoofing. This implementation is verified using SAST (to ensure SslMode=VerifyFull or equivalent is used in the driver) and Secret Scanning to ensure the database endpoint URL remains confidential</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7.1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S77f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130ea5b90cdd-b31b-4e97-a152-48ecdda0f4703d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>a5b90cdd-b31b-4e97-a152-48ecdda0f470</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>30</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e:a5b90cdd-b31b-4e97-a152-48ecdda0f470:3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:31:45.5839695+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Source Data Store Product Catalog </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Identity-Based Access Control and Database Authentication. The Order Database is configured to only accept write-requests from a specific service account associated with the Product Catalog. Every request must include a Signed JWT (JSON Web Token) that proves the sender's identity. Additionally, we use IP Whitelisting at the database firewall level to ensure only the Product Catalog’s container can establish a connection. This implementation is verified using SAST (to ensure credentials are not hardcoded) and Secret Scanning to ensure the database tokens are managed securely via GitHub Secrets.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860fcf34ff4-c76f-4dc8-965f-fb6d384d451065bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695a</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>fcf34ff4-c76f-4dc8-965f-fb6d384d4510</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>26</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860:fcf34ff4-c76f-4dc8-965f-fb6d384d4510:65bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695a</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:29:29.1935251+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Source Data Store Cart Management </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Service-to-Service Authentication using Mutual TLS (mTLS) and API Keys. The Payment Processing service validates the client certificate of the Cart Management service before accepting any request. Additionally, a unique, encrypted Source ID is included in the header of every request, which is verified against our internal identity registry. This ensures that only the authentic Cart Management service can initiate payment workflows. This implementation is verified using SAST (to check for hardcoded secrets) and DAST (to test unauthorized access attempts).</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>65bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695a</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7.1b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860fcf34ff4-c76f-4dc8-965f-fb6d384d451065bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695a</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>fcf34ff4-c76f-4dc8-965f-fb6d384d4510</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>27</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860:fcf34ff4-c76f-4dc8-965f-fb6d384d4510:65bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695a</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:30:02.6722514+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Destination Data Store Payment Processing </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by enforcing TLS 1.3 with Certificate Pinning. The application is configured to strictly verify the destination's SSL certificate against our internal Root Certificate Authority (CA). Additionally, the Payment Processing endpoint URL is stored in a Read-Only Environment Variable managed via GitHub Secrets, preventing an attacker from redirecting the traffic via DNS poisoning or local configuration changes. This implementation is verified using SAST (to ensure certificate validation is mandatory in the code) and Cloud Security Posture Management (CSPM) to audit the network path.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>b89b8dbf-d64b-46fa-a906-f2ebb8b70860</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>65bdc55d-67c4-4caa-b22b-19620a20695a</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7.1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S73d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330dc953c52-9138-4e62-99e3-ccf0b6ff03357f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>dc953c52-9138-4e62-99e3-ccf0b6ff0335</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>32</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330:dc953c52-9138-4e62-99e3-ccf0b6ff0335:7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:32:51.4128899+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Source Data Store Order Database </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Database-to-Service Authentication using mTLS (Mutual TLS). The Product Catalog service requires the Order Database to present a valid Client Certificate signed by our internal Certificate Authority (CA) before any data is accepted. Furthermore, all data retrieved is validated against a known Schema Definition to prevent data injection attacks. This implementation is verified using SAST (Static Analysis) to ensure that the database client library is configured to reject unauthenticated or untrusted connections and Secret Scanning to ensure certificate private keys are stored securely in GitHub Secrets</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Response </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7.13d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330dc953c52-9138-4e62-99e3-ccf0b6ff03357f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>dc953c52-9138-4e62-99e3-ccf0b6ff0335</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>33</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330:dc953c52-9138-4e62-99e3-ccf0b6ff0335:7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:34:51.8023207+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Destination Data Store Product Catalog </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Product Catalog  may be spoofed by an attacker and this may lead to data being written to the attacker's targThis threat is mitigated by implementing Service Identity Verification through mTLS (Mutual TLS). The sending service (Order Database/Management) is configured to verify the TLS Certificate of the Product Catalog destination against a trusted internal Root CA. Additionally, we use HSTS (HTTP Strict Transport Security) to prevent protocol downgrade attacks. This ensures that data cannot be rerouted to an unverified attacker's target. This implementation is verified using SAST to ensure the connection logic enforces strict certificate validation and SCA (Snyk) to ensure the underlying communication libraries are free from vulnerabilitieset instead of Product Catalog . Consider using a standard authentication mechanism to identify the destination data store.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Response </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>7f76dc00-1bdd-41d9-ab4f-3b81e6d1130e</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7.1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>D2534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718171252e0-f28f-4549-b41c-7259d1dbcc543d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>171252e0-f28f-4549-b41c-7259d1dbcc54</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>42</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718:171252e0-f28f-4549-b41c-7259d1dbcc54:3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:37:12.1602215+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Potential Excessive Resource Consumption for Host  or Order Database </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Denial Of Service</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Denial of Service happens when the process or a datastore is not able to service incoming requests or perform up to spec.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Resource Quotas and Connection Throttling. At the infrastructure level, the Host is protected by Docker/Kubernetes Resource Limits (CPU and Memory caps) to prevent a single container from exhausting the entire node. For the Order Database, we have implemented Connection Pooling with a maximum limit and Query Timeouts to prevent long-running or malicious queries from deadlocking the system. Additionally, an API Gateway enforces Rate Limiting (requests per second) to block volumetric Denial of Service (DoS) attacks. These controls are verified through Load Testing and Chaos Engineering to ensure system stability under stress.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>D2</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>I233d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330dd553d5b-0575-4a6e-8a63-df9369c19939534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>dd553d5b-0575-4a6e-8a63-df9369c19939</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>38</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330:dd553d5b-0575-4a6e-8a63-df9369c19939:534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:36:16.2169225+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Weak Access Control for a Resource</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Information Disclosure</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Information disclosure happens when the information can be read by an unauthorized party.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC) and Encryption at Rest. Access to the Order Database is restricted via Least Privilege IAM roles, ensuring only the 'Order Management' service can query transaction data. All sensitive PII (Personally Identifiable Information) within the database is encrypted using AES-256. Furthermore, we have enabled Database Auditing to log all read/write attempts. This is verified using SCA (Snyk) to ensure database drivers are secure and Secret Scanning (Gitleaks) to ensure database administrative credentials are never exposed in the source code</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>I23</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S73d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330dd553d5b-0575-4a6e-8a63-df9369c19939534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>dd553d5b-0575-4a6e-8a63-df9369c19939</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>37</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330:dd553d5b-0575-4a6e-8a63-df9369c19939:534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:35:37.8589638+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Source Data Store Order Database </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Service-to-Host Authentication via Mutual TLS (mTLS) and Signed Payloads. The Host environment is configured to only accept data streams from the Order Database that are encrypted and signed with a unique certificate issued by our internal Certificate Authority (CA). We have also implemented IP-to-Service Binding within the container orchestrator (e.g., Kubernetes Network Policies) to ensure that only the verified Database IP can communicate with the Host management port. This is verified using SAST to ensure the host-client configuration enforces certificate validation and Secret Scanning to protect the CA certificates.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7.1534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718171252e0-f28f-4549-b41c-7259d1dbcc543d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>171252e0-f28f-4549-b41c-7259d1dbcc54</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>41</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718:171252e0-f28f-4549-b41c-7259d1dbcc54:3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:36:46.9588261+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Destination Data Store Order Database </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by enforcing TLS 1.3 with Server Certificate Validation. The application is configured with a 'Verify-Full' SSL mode, meaning it checks that the Order Database's certificate is signed by our trusted internal Certificate Authority (CA) and that the hostname matches the certificate's Common Name (CN). Additionally, the database connection string is stored as a GitHub Secret and injected into the environment at runtime, preventing local configuration tampering. This implementation is verified using SAST (Bandit) to ensure the database client library does not allow 'skip-verify' or insecure connections</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>3d40dfb3-ebd2-4e08-bda0-a1e55249f330</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7.1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f7cad701f-e0b3-46ad-9ce3-6b98bc2114a1534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>7cad701f-e0b3-46ad-9ce3-6b98bc2114a1</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>43</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f:7cad701f-e0b3-46ad-9ce3-6b98bc2114a1:534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:38:03.1153028+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Source Data Store Non Relational Database</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Database Identity Verification using Mutual TLS (mTLS). The Host is configured to verify the database's certificate against a trusted internal Root CA before accepting any incoming data stream. Additionally, we use Secure Environment Variables within GitHub Secrets to store the database's unique fingerprint (SHA-256 hash), ensuring the Host only connects to the verified, pre-defined instance. This implementation is verified using SAST (Bandit) to ensure the connection logic enforces strict certificate validation and Gitleaks to ensure no database credentials or connection strings are exposed in the codebase.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>I23a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f7cad701f-e0b3-46ad-9ce3-6b98bc2114a1534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>7cad701f-e0b3-46ad-9ce3-6b98bc2114a1</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>44</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f:7cad701f-e0b3-46ad-9ce3-6b98bc2114a1:534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:38:36.4661203+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Weak Access Control for a Resource</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Information Disclosure</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Information disclosure happens when the information can be read by an unauthorized party.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) and Encryption at Rest. The Non-Relational Database is configured to deny all requests by default (Deny-All), allowing access only to specific service accounts with the 'Read-Only' or 'Read-Write' roles as required. All sensitive data fields are encrypted using AES-256. Additionally, the database is hosted within a Private Subnet with no public internet access, and all queries are audited via cloud logging. This is verified using Snyk (SCA) to ensure no vulnerable database drivers are used and Gitleaks to ensure database administrative passwords are not committed to the repository</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Request </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>I23</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7.1534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc92571890187a3e-79bb-4da7-b86b-3d1393dd11f6a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>90187a3e-79bb-4da7-b86b-3d1393dd11f6</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>45</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718:90187a3e-79bb-4da7-b86b-3d1393dd11f6:a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:39:07.3250218+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Destination Data Store Non Relational Database</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Server-Side TLS Certificate Validation. The application is configured with a 'Verify-Full' SSL mode, requiring the Non-Relational Database to present a certificate signed by our internal Certificate Authority (CA). The application also verifies the Subject Alternative Name (SAN) to ensure the hostname matches the intended database endpoint. Additionally, the destination URL is stored in GitHub Secrets and injected into the container environment at runtime, preventing attackers from redirecting traffic through local configuration tampering. This is verified using SAST (Bandit) to ensure the connection strings do not allow 'insecure' or 'allow-all' SSL configurations</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Response </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7.1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>D2534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc92571890187a3e-79bb-4da7-b86b-3d1393dd11f6a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>90187a3e-79bb-4da7-b86b-3d1393dd11f6</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>46</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718:90187a3e-79bb-4da7-b86b-3d1393dd11f6:a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:40:20.9535449+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Potential Excessive Resource Consumption for Host  or Non Relational Database</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Denial Of Service</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Denial of Service happens when the process or a datastore is not able to service incoming requests or perform up to spec.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated by implementing Docker Resource Limits and OS-level Throttling. Even without Kubernetes, we enforce CPU and Memory limits directly in the Docker Compose file (mem_limit and cpus) to prevent the Non-Relational Database from consuming all Host resources. At the database level, we have configured Maximum Connection Limits and Query Execution Timeouts to prevent deadlocks and resource exhaustion. Additionally, the Host OS is configured with ulimit settings to restrict the number of open files and processes. These controls are verified through Manual Load Testing and monitored via Docker Stats to ensure the system remains responsive under high load</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>Response </a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>534e1165-8832-43da-9cbc-618ccc925718</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>a7ba97a2-1fcd-4889-8f36-1fcc9f267e1f</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>D2</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>BSca38ef3f-9a7b-4c48-a9a0-ce11518635f82d99c213-a686-49ff-ab60-7169dbec92355cfb0897-5c33-4342-a5fe-159ed85894cbf4a60cc3-fc3f-4834-ac82-a17b28ee6e0e</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy>DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP</b:ChangedBy><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>cee68ad4-716d-4fa2-b0f6-50e6b71d49f3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>5cfb0897-5c33-4342-a5fe-159ed85894cb</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>47</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>2d99c213-a686-49ff-ab60-7169dbec9235:5cfb0897-5c33-4342-a5fe-159ed85894cb:f4a60cc3-fc3f-4834-ac82-a17b28ee6e0e</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>2026-01-16T15:42:00.5993049+05:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Nonstandard threat to describe user specific conditions</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>User-defined</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>User-Defined Threat Category to describe custom threat types</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value/></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>StateInformation</a:Key><a:Value>This threat is mitigated through Host Hardening and Docker Container Isolation. The host machine (DESKTOP-8TCFF7Q\HP) is secured by a firewall that blocks all ports except those strictly necessary for the application. To prevent a 'container breakout' where an attacker could take control of the host, the application and database run as non-root users within Docker. We have also disabled Docker's default bridge network in favor of a custom private network, ensuring that services are not exposed to the local host network unnecessarily. This is verified by Secret Scanning to ensure no host SSH keys are leaked and Snyk to audit the container base images for OS-level vulnerabilities</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>No Flow</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>2d99c213-a686-49ff-ab60-7169dbec9235</b:SourceGuid><b:State>Mitigated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>f4a60cc3-fc3f-4834-ac82-a17b28ee6e0e</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>BSca38ef3f-9a7b-4c48-a9a0-ce11518635f8</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>true</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB></ThreatInstances><ThreatGenerationEnabled>true</ThreatGenerationEnabled><Validations xmlns:a="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"/><Version>4.3</Version><KnowledgeBase z:Id="i30" xmlns:a="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase" xmlns:z="http://schemas.microsoft.com/2003/10/Serialization/"><a:GenericElements><a:ElementType><a:IsExtension>false</a:IsExtension><a:Attributes><a:Attribute><a:IsExtension>false</a:IsExtension><a:AttributeValues xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Interfaces"><b:Value>Not Selected</b:Value><b:Value>Managed</b:Value><b:Value>Unmanaged</b:Value></a:AttributeValues><a:DisplayName>Code Type</a:DisplayName><a:Inheritance>Virtual</a:Inheritance><a:Mode>Dynamic</a:Mode><a:Name>codeType</a:Name><a:Type>List</a:Type></a:Attribute><a:Attribute><a:IsExtension>false</a:IsExtension><a:AttributeValues xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Interfaces"><b:Value>Not Selected</b:Value><b:Value>Kernel</b:Value><b:Value>System</b:Value><b:Value>Network Service</b:Value><b:Value>Local Service</b:Value><b:Value>Administrator</b:Value><b:Value>Standard User With Elevation</b:Value><b:Value>Standard User Without Elevation</b:Value><b:Value>Windows Store App</b:Value></a:AttributeValues><a:DisplayName>Running As</a:DisplayName><a:Inheritance>Virtual</a:Inheritance><a:Mode>Dynamic</a:Mode><a:Name>runningAs</a:Name><a:Type>List</a:Type></a:Attribute><a:Attribute><a:IsExtension>false</a:IsExtension><a:AttributeValues xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Interfaces"><b:Value>Not Selected</b:Value><b:Value>AppContainer</b:Value><b:Value>Low Integrity Level</b:Value><b:Value>Microsoft Office Isolated Conversion Environment (MOICE)</b:Value><b:Value>Sandbox</b:Value></a:AttributeValues><a:DisplayName>Isolation Level</a:DisplayName><a:Inheritance>Virtual</a:Inheritance><a:Mode>Dynamic</a:Mode><a:Name>Isolation</a:Name><a:Type>List</a:Type></a:Attribute><a:Attribute><a:IsExtension>false</a:IsExtension><a:AttributeValues xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModelin